e the women lived and he  welcomed us and then he told us that it  was that was very serious business to be  here because they had kept out so many  men to let these girls in  this world all ten girls and that we  should be and the it was very important  that we finished the course because we'd  kept out all those men and so we should  be very serious about that and finish  our three-year program and then he added  and I've never sorted this out totally  he was a wonderful Southern Christian  gentleman and I loved him dearly he said  it was very hard to decide among all the  women who had applied and so we very  carefully went over your applications  and we looked very carefully at all your  pictures and we picked the ten women we  figured would stay the course  well you know  you see it takes a while for him to rest  and it took a while for us to register  and I don't remember that we reacted  particularly I mean we were this is 1950  we were very nice girls who had been  well brought up to be happy with the  system and you didn't get angry at a  nice professor in front of you even  though you weren't quite sure what he  meant by that govern anyway um it was a  wonderful time here the Golden Age of  faculty as they frequently say it was  the doctor bein neighbor Calhoun Napier  the Pope two popes anyway it was a  wonderful wonderful faculty and we loved  it and we thrived under them and but  there were no women faculty I'm not sure  whether we ever verbally regretted that  out loud or even noticed it you know at  that point that wasn't in the  consciousness there was no technical  women center the 301 prospect street  down there at the corner Edwards in  profit all the women lived there 28 or  30 of us so forgiving the three-year  program and so it was a women center of  a kind and there was lots of support  there but not I don't remember any  conversations about consciousness  raising or about roles of women or about  identity or about what's wrong with the  school that it doesn't have more  equipment in there I don't remember any  of that we were serious students who  were thankful to be here and we pretty  much pretty much thrived on there was  political consciousness raising though I  remember that they said tell some  anecdotes I remember this one the day I  arrived at 301 was bringing my suitcases  in the front door up the steps and at  the top on the second floor I heard one  voice saying energetically to somebody  else obviously a first-year woman's  student that's the other woman was  saying to her but you can't be a  Republican and be a Christian  and there I was a nice little Republican  from the little white suburban town in  New Jersey I thought oh my there was  also a an organization a group of  students and one faculty or two who did  what was called religion at the news  desk and then Austin was part of that  and others who dealt with all the news  and political news in the world so there  was consciousness about that and there  was some diversity among women students  in my class of 10 there was one woman  from Puerto Rico and one of Japanese  background from Hawaii and in the rest  there were some women of color and the  rest of the among the rest of the women  who were there in our Bernice cozy  pulley and and all in those early years  so we weren't all we didn't all look  alike which was great um ok now I'm  going to jump from the early 50s to the  70s I have two minutes footsteps I'm  sorry the main thing you need to know  about the early 70s was the massive sea  change in this school it was not just  that that of the first woman faculty  came but also the first Roman Catholic  faculty Margaret Farley and renowned  also Berkeley divinity school joined us  so for the first time it was a  university Divinity School and a church  related seminary connected and then  there was suddenly an advocate for women  and nobody quite knew what that was  about except the women started to apply  those were the years when the Holy  Spirit blew the doors open of every  seminary in this country and the women  who had been waiting came pouring in  everywhere church-related seminaries and  other schools across the country in 71  72 73 74 there were 28 women in the  student body when I started as well it  was called advocate for women I quickly  said could we call that advocate for  students so they don't think women are  needy and helpless and whatever I so it  came advocate for students there were 28  the next year there were 52 the next  year there were 90 women  the next rig went over a hundred and I  stopped hatching all the simplifications  the men faculty said what are you doing  you out r you were not allowed to  recruit there was no recruiting at Yale  and nearly up to the early seventies  newton and so you can't be recruiting i  said i'm not recruiting the holy space  blowing them through the door and I just  I just welcomed them I'm gonna stop  there and we get to back to some other  things now be plenty of time also to  share more but now we turn to Emily  towns who is one of the newer members of  the faculty and also the academic dean I  want to start by thanking both Margaret  and Joan it's um I wouldn't be here if  you hadn't been so  and i also want to put faces to i'm  going to do this in stages so don't jump  up before I call you put faces to the  women full-time women faculty who were  here and some of you I've seen and i'm  not sure i've identified everyone but if  we could start with jennifer just stand  and say your name so folks can see you  anyone else in this session section over  in here keep standing keep standing more  back this way I know Nora tubs Tisdale  was here but she may have left Yolanda  okay and adjunct faculty women faculty  Shannon other folks anyone else up okay  and i'm not sure if any of our visiting  women faculty are here today I just  think it's important to see the faces  Martha you I think it's important to see  the faces you hear the names often but  some of you may not always see the faces  and know them so I just wanted to see  everyone to see who I work with along  with the men but also  I'm going to do some little bit from  some of the things that Margaret's  address touched off with me and I'm  going to start with something some of  you have heard me do before but most of  you have not and it's a soliloquy from  the movie daughters of the dust by Julie  dash it's the story of a Gullah Sea  Island family it's Gulliver on this  island on the islands off of South  Carolina and they have lived on these  islands basically untouched in this  movie it was made or set in the early  late 1800s Early 1900s and this family  is going to move to the mainland and up  to New York and so there's a little bit  of consternation going on with what's  going to happen and there is one seen  near the end of the movie in which EULA  who is one of the granddaughters of Nana  pisat has been raped and the audience  knows that the person who everybody  knows who raped her was a white man  she's with child and no one knows whose  child it is is it her husband's child  Eli or is it the the child of the result  of this rape the narrator of the story  is the child who's not yet born and the  child is their child july and EULA's  child well there's another woman who  comes home to the island yellow Mary who  was a prostitute and she turned to that  life after she had been raped but her  husband left her so she had no other  resources on the mainland so she turns  to prostitution and the women are  ostracizing her and EULA says this to  them there's going to be all kinds of  words in life to take let's not be  afraid to take them we  serve them because we're all good women  do you do you know who you are and what  you've become you're the daughters of  those old dusty Tings Nana carries in  her tin can we carry too many scars from  the past our scars own us a mother's  scars our sister skies our daughters Gus  thick hard ugly scars so no one can ever  pass through to hurt us again let's live  our lives outside of the folds of those  old wounds now when I started it was 77  it wasn't here it was in another school  West  in the city of Chicago and it was very  clear we had inherited some fairly thick  scars that we didn't even know what  those scars were all about we just knew  that they were present and so we began  to think through as students how do we  do that how do we deal with something  that we can't yet quite name but we know  is present and we can see the women in  front of us responding to this and  trying to not make their struggle hours  but be true to the struggles that we saw  in ourselves so one of the things that I  think that Margaret challenges us to do  is how do you pass on the need to  remember that there is a struggle to not  make the struggles that have come in the  past the ones that this generation is  dealing with let them have their own  scars and wounds and work but to talk  together so I first think we have to  recognize that there is one and second  we must have deep intergenerational  conversations and I want to stress  conversations because so often those of  us who are old I'd like to tell those of  us who are younger what they should be  doing and they listen about as well as  we did so we should probably learn a  little bit from our own hardheadedness  that the way to help people understand  more is by conversations not telling  third these conversations have to move  from just being talked to analysis and  strategy sessions and then finally we've  got to act now many women come knowing  the classics of contemporary voices here  at Yale  a cup of classics and the contemporary  voices and I am happy to say some days  that it's a joyful moment when one or  two or several come into my office or  catch me in the hallway or find me in  the common room or see me in the servery  or catch me after chapel hill when they  know I might be particularly vulnerable  and say I'm taking this class and I want  to know why there are no women in it  that we have to read I think that is a  mark of movement and it's not just women  who come and ask that question it's men  as well we have moved a good bit so what  I think we are about now is helping them  go deeper if they come knowing this from  their undergraduate careers or where  they've been before then the job of Yale  is to help them go deeper so we must be  a faculty who can help those do so with  precision and rigor in other words to be  wide es at its best it's scholarly and  ministerial best we need to be about  helping women develop a belief in the  gifts God has placed in them with  humility but without apology and I think  that's the harder thing to pull off too  many of us come in here being humble not  enough without apology so we're not only  dancing Sarah's circle I know Margaret  you're standing so I'm almost done we're  not only dance sarah circles now a wide  yes we're also learning how to do a  shake dance for the postmodern age that  needs more heart and soul in a world  that gets more sterile and indifferent  each year so as one of the old black  women of my grandmother's generation  used to say to her friends  girl it's time to get a moving and so we  are  and now I turn it back over to tullys  Margaret Joan and Emily I cannot thank  you enough both personally and on behalf  of all who are gathered here for the  wisdom that you've offered us in a very  succinct kind of way the things you've  reminded us that we know and the things  that you have challenged us to relearn  once again in this time we'd like to  offer some time for you to for us  together as a community to reflect on  what we've heard and also on some of the  questions that have been raised Margaret  raised in her presentation the question  about how is this home or where have we  found home both here but also in the  ministries and lives to which God has  called us and for which God has  empowered us what have been challenges  what have been the graces you'll see  both in the booklet but also on your  yellow yellow program on the flip side  of it are three questions in your life  in your life at yts and beyond what  would you identify is the greatest  challenge you faced what would you  identify is the greatest race you've  known and where have you found a home in  ministry in God's heart in both I'm  brought you to take just a few moments  to reflect on these questions it's not  too much time is not enough but  certainly enough time to go really deep  but to begin to think about this is  these questions shaping our time  together and here at the Women's reunion  and then as the spirit moves you turn to  someone next to you and just partner up  for just a few minutes and share an  answer to one of those questions about  grace about challenge about finding home  these questions are mainly to help help  us think about the time that we have and  also the time that God will call us to  when we leave from this place yet once  again to face the challenges of our  lives and ministries and also to find  the Grace and the home that God offers  us wherever we are so take a moment  especially for the introverts to be  introverted and think about think about  sure what you might do with these  questions and then turn to someone near  you someone suggested everybody turn to  their right but if we do that then that  doesn't actually work so figure out how  you're going to turn to somebody next to  you and find someone to share this with  preferably somebody across the  generation perhaps we'll come back  together with the singing of you be  kotti toss but take some time now
 

